      \field{abstract}{The application of artificial intelligence has become extremely popular nowadays. Whereas “{AI} is a foundational catalyst for digital business”, in reality most organizations struggle to scale their {AI} pilots into enterprise-wide production, which limits the ability to realize {AI}'s potential business value. To be more precise, organizations “are discovering '{AI} pilot paradox', where launching pilots is deceptively easy but deploying them into production is notoriously challenging”. One reason for this situation is “that the risks and challenges that {AI} poses, including codifying and reinforcing unfair biases and infringing on human rights such as privacy, are undeniable”. Thus, these issues are causing public concern and raising questions about the trustworthiness and regulation of {AI} applications. “If {AI} systems do not prove to be worthy of trust, their widespread acceptance and adoption will be hindered, and the potentially vast societal and economic benefits will not be fully realized”. Related to the overall goal of this research (Trustworthiness in {AI}-based Big Data Analysis), this research is focusing on the definition of intelligence, artificial intelligence, trust, and trustworthiness first. Afterwards and to integrate trustworthy artificial intelligence into the whole big data analysis process, relevant related work will be outlined and discussed. Based on these results, a conceptual reference model for trustworthiness in {AI}-based big data analysis ({TAI}-{BDM} Reference Model) will be introduced and discussed.}
